# TO_BYTES -- 1 row, DOWN FROM 3, AND THE ONE THAT REMAINS IS A DIFFERENT AND LARGER DEFECT.
# `to_bytes_f32i` and `to_bytes_f16i` ARE EXACT: `bytes_of` was truncating `8 - sz*2` characters
# from the LEFT of a ten-character `0x%08x`, which is big-endian, keeps the `0x`, and cannot widen
# past four bytes, and it is now one hex PAIR PER BYTE, LOW BYTE FIRST. But `to_bytes_vop2` MOVED
# rather than cleared -- port `` -> `000a06140000` -- and the reason is NOT the byte order any
# more, it is THE WIDTH OF THE WORD ITSELF.
#   CPython: `raw = 0x4814060a00`, `bit_length() == 39`, `_base_size == 6`,
#            `raw.to_bytes(6, "little").hex() == "000a06144800"`. The `48` in CPython's answer is
#            `sdst` at bits [41:36] (`Reg(8) >> 1 << 36`) and the `8` is `op_sel` at [35:34].
#   THE PORT: `Res` IS `Res{v: U32, s: String}` and `bf_set.placed` shifts with `U32.shln`, so
#            EVERY BIT AT INDEX >= 32 IS DISCARDED. A VOP2 word is 42 bits wide.
# SO THIS ROW IS THE ONLY ONE LEFT IN THE CLASS AND IT IS NOT A TEXT PROBLEM: an instruction wider
# than 32 bits encodes with its HIGH FIELDS MISSING, and that is every VOP3 and every MFMA.
# WIDENING `raw` IS NOT DONE HERE -- it is a change to `Res`, `bf_mask`, `bf_set`, `hex8` and
# `rws`, and it would move every `raw_*` and `bfs` row, because `hex8` MASKS TO 32 BITS ON BOTH
# SIDES (the oracle's `hex8` is `f"{v & U32:#010x}"`, and CPython's own `f"{x:#010x}"` is NOT a
# mask). That shared truncation is itself an unrecorded carve-out and it is why `raw_vop2_all`
# reads `|0x14060a00` on both sides where CPython's word is `0x4814060a00`.
to_bytes_vop2	000a06144800	000a06140000